# Build 356.
#
# This file is not about publishing anywhere. It exists because `apply-build`
# uses git locally to install a reviewed patch, and its deletion rule is stated
# in its own prompt:
#
#     Only the tracked paths above will be deleted by the reviewed Git patch.
#     Untracked/ignored local files and directories are not deletion candidates.
#
# The durable calibration table is host state: written by the suite, per
# machine, tens of minutes to compute, never the same on two hosts. While it is
# a tracked path, both possible builds are wrong -- one that contains it
# overwrites the host's table with the packager's (build 352, 42m47s lost) and
# one that does not contain it deletes the host's table (build 354, 42m47s lost
# again). Ignored is the only state with neither failure.
#
# `package_release.py` has expected a .gitignore at the root since the project
# was packaged and there was never one, which is the same defect class as the
# LICENSE that build 351 found missing after every wheel build had warned about
# it.
calibration/probe_calibration.json

# Runtime state. `var/` is documented as safe to delete; `instance/` holds the
# folder-local PostgreSQL cluster and its socket.
var/
instance/
# The folder a study bundle is put into to seed a study from it (869): the operator's files, never the build's.
import/
# The PostgreSQL slice tests' own folders (`tests/integration/test_postgres_native.py`, `test_operator_path.py`),
# removed when they pass, and tools/study_twice.py's two folders, removed when it ends.
.pytest_slice_var_*/
.pytest_operator_var_*/
.study_twice_*/

# Build products and caches.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.venv/
target/
*.so

# Build 364, from an applied build that deleted a live host's credentials.
#
# The deletion rule quoted above cuts both ways, and only one direction was
# ever written down. A path that `package_release.py` deliberately keeps out of
# an archive, and that a host has on disk, is a tracked path that the incoming
# build does not contain -- so the patch deletes it. Everything the packager
# excludes must therefore be ignored here as well, or the exclusion becomes a
# deletion the first time a correctly cut archive is applied.
#
# `.env.postgres` was excluded from archives and not ignored. It holds the only
# copy of the folder-local cluster's password, which README section 5 calls out
# as the one thing whose loss locks the folder out of its own database. Nothing
# caught it because no archive had been cut by the release tool in a long time:
# 362 was tarred by hand and carried the host's own file back to it, so the
# rule never fired. 363 was the first correctly cut archive and it fired
# immediately.
#
# Recovery, if this happens again and there is no backup: the cluster survives,
# so `python tools/postgres_instance.py reset-credentials` mints a new password
# and applies it, keeping the campaign. Do not delete `instance/`.
#
# `tests/unit/test_build_364_archive_and_ignore_agree.py` compares this file
# against the packager's exclusion lists, so the two cannot drift apart again.
.env.postgres

# Coverage data and the caches and build products the packager excludes. None
# of these is dangerous to lose; they are here because "excluded from the
# archive" and "ignored" must be the same set, and an exception nobody can
# state is how the important one went unnoticed among them.
.coverage
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
build/
dist/
venv/
*.dump

# The diagram exporter's npm install (tools/diagrams/export_mermaid.js)
tools/diagrams/node_modules/
tools/diagrams/package-lock.json

# 688 (the build 685 review; CLI-10): host state the release now keeps out, ignored as everything it excludes is (build
# 364's rule above): the diagram tool's installed packages, a calibration table half written, and the copy an adoption
# keeps of the table it replaced.
node_modules/
*.tmp
*.before-adopt
*.egg-info/
